package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// Types shared by `spawn`, `tool-discovery` and `tool-loading`, and the
/// rules every implementer of them keeps. It has no free functions, so
/// importing it grants nothing; each power is its own import. `approval` is
/// served by the implementer of `spawn` and `tool-loading`. Unstable: enable
/// `agent-spawn`, plus `agent-run` and `session-status`, whose types these
/// use. Rationale is in `docs/spawn/DESIGN-NOTES.md`.
///
/// The implementer is whatever serves these imports: a host plugin, a policy
/// layer the host installs in front of it, or a test double. The caller is
/// the component the import is bound to.
///
/// One session per workload. These imports are served only to a workload
/// bound to exactly one session for its whole life; a host that binds
/// several sessions to one workload answers `disabled`.
///
/// Caller identity. Everything these interfaces tie to a caller (approval
/// digests, coalescing, spawn tickets, the pending-approval cap, the denial
/// cooldown, repeat tracking, the session ceiling, loaded toolsets, a
/// child's owner) uses the workload incarnation (minted when the workload is
/// created, kept across rollouts and restarts), the component's name in that
/// workload (so siblings are distinct callers), and the session id. Never an
/// instance id. Namespace and workload name are display and audit metadata.
/// A policy layer must be installed by the host and pass the original
/// caller through.
///
/// Reach. A request's read reach is the union, across the agent and every
/// toolset, of its read grants: volumes, secrets, interfaces the
/// implementer classifies as reading, and the model alias. Its egress reach
/// is the union of allowed hosts and interfaces classified as writing. A
/// read-write volume counts as both. Tool annotations (`read-only` and the
/// rest in `tool-types`) are publisher claims and never feed reach,
/// admission or the ceiling.
///
/// Ceiling. A caller's ceiling has two parts that never mix:
///
/// - Own grants: those of the caller's own component (for a child, its
///   `spawn-request.grants`). A new grant fits when one of these contains
///   it. A volume fits a mount of the same storage at the same path or
///   below, and read-write fits only a read-write mount. An allowed host
///   fits by the containment rules in `docs/spawn/DESIGN-NOTES.md`
///   (`*.github.com` contains `api.github.com`, not `github.com`; a bare
///   host covers that host on any scheme's default port, never another
///   port). A model alias fits one the caller was granted. A grant the
///   implementer cannot see or compare does not fit.
/// - Toolset entries: the toolsets the caller holds, each at an exact digest
///   and admission snapshot (declared toolsets, a child's spawned toolsets,
///   and the session ceiling). A toolset fits only as an exact match for an
///   entry. An entry's reach never counts as the caller's own, so approving
///   one toolset never lends its reach to another component.
///
/// Session ceiling. A person's approval of a toolset load adds an entry to
/// the session's ceiling, keyed to the caller, the `toolset-spec` digest and
/// the admission snapshot reviewed. A later identical load (after a
/// set-aside, an unbind, a rollback or a failed rollout) is within the
/// ceiling and needs no prompt.
///
/// - Re-checked at each use: in the step that admits it, the implementer
///   resolves the request again, compares it with the entry's snapshot and
///   checks current policy. Any difference puts the toolset outside the
///   ceiling.
/// - Never widens: an entry admits its exact snapshot only, and only a
///   person's load approval adds one. A spawn approval adds none (see
///   Children).
/// - Only the person removes an entry, in the host's UI, never through an
///   import. Removal withdraws everything resting on that entry: a pending
///   approval for it resolves `expired`, an unconsumed spawn ticket naming
///   it is void, a rollout adding it is not published, it is unbound, a call
///   to it in flight is cancelled, and any descendant admission resting on
///   it is withdrawn (its toolset unbound, or the child `revoked`).
///   Admissions resting on other entries are untouched. Each removal
///   advances the session's revocation generation; a step that started
///   before a removal sees the mismatch, re-checks that its own entry still
///   exists, and proceeds only if it does, otherwise answering `denied`. A
///   removal also starts the denial cooldown for that toolset in that
///   session.
/// - Session-scoped and audited: it lives in the session's state, moves with
///   the session between hosts, and ends with it. The host's UI lists every
///   entry, bound or not.
///
/// Attenuation. A caller may request beyond its ceiling but never grant it.
/// The implementer is the sole grantor (by policy, an auditor the host
/// installs, or a person) and returns `needs-approval`. Within the ceiling
/// it admits at once only digests in the operator's catalog or matching a
/// toolset entry; any other digest goes to a grantor.
///
/// Children. A child session's reach never exceeds what its ancestors hold
/// plus what a person approved for that exact child.
///
/// - The child agent component's own `grants` must fit the parent's own
///   grants, and its model alias one the parent was granted, or the spawn is
///   `denied`. Reach the parent lacks goes in a toolset. A catalog
///   `definition` is expanded into its component, alias, grants and
///   toolsets, and admitted by the same rules.
/// - A child's toolset that fits the parent's ceiling is admitted, resting
///   on the grant or entry it matched. One beyond it goes to a grantor only
///   if it fits the ceiling of every ancestor above the parent (none, for a
///   root parent); otherwise it is `denied`.
/// - A spawn approval is a one-time ticket for one child (one idempotency
///   key) and adds nothing to the parent's ceiling. The child holds the
///   approved toolset as an entry bound to the spawn's digest, so it carries
///   the exact output, budget and powers approved.
/// - Re-checked as the tree changes. For each of a child's grants and
///   entries, the implementer records the authority it rested on (an
///   ancestor's grant or entry, or a person's approval of this child), and at
///   each run acceptance and child load re-checks it against that authority
///   as it stands and against current policy. An entry resting on an
///   approval of this child is checked against that approval (its snapshot,
///   and that the toolset was not removed) and against the ancestors above
///   the parent it was checked against when approved. A toolset that no
///   longer fits is withdrawn; a child whose own grants no longer fit takes
///   no more runs (`revoked`). Changes take effect at the next acceptance or
///   dispatch, never mid-call.
/// - A child's loads follow the same rule: beyond its ceiling but within
///   every ancestor's goes to a grantor, labelled with the child's place in
///   the tree; beyond any ancestor's is `denied`.
///
/// Dispatch, never linking. A toolset is one component in the agent's own
/// workload, with its own grants, that offers one or more tools: a tool
/// provider (exports `cosmonic:agent/tools`) or a typed tool (exports its
/// own interfaces). Its exports are reached only through the host's
/// dispatch, never linked to the agent or a sibling; its imports are served
/// only by its own grants, and it egresses as itself. A host whose runtime
/// would link a toolset's exports to a sibling refuses that toolset with
/// `invalid-request`.
///
/// Authorship. A child's final reply is the only child-authored text the
/// caller sees, and it can carry anything the child read. Progress, errors,
/// reasons, and the outcome's `finish`, `model` and counts are the
/// implementer's own. A child's conversation is visible only to a person,
/// in the host's own view; what a person tells a child may reach the parent
/// in a later reply. A child still steers how much work it does, how its run
/// ends, whether its outcome exceeds `output.max-bytes`, and its timing.
/// Nothing here closes covert or timing channels or blocks prompt
/// injection.
///
/// Credentials never pass through these interfaces. Models, secrets and
/// credential references are named; the implementer resolves them and keeps
/// values out of every agent's memory. A spawned agent's own component never
/// receives secrets.
///
/// Audit. The implementer records every admission (through the catalog, a
/// toolset entry or a grantor), refusal, removal, withdrawal, set-aside and
/// unbind, naming what each rested on.
///
/// Authority to decide. The grantor is a person, or policy a person wrote.
/// A spawned agent never holds approval authority, and an auditor is
/// installed by host policy, never spawned.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface spawn-types {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use inference-types.{json};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use run-types.{run-outcome, run-counts};

    /// A component image pinned by content digest, so a moving tag cannot
    /// change what was approved.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record component-ref {
        /// An OCI reference such as `ghcr.io/acme/agents/coder:1.2.0`, for
        /// display; `digest` is authoritative. A person is shown the catalog
        /// name for a catalogued digest, and otherwise this reference only
        /// once the implementer confirms the repository serves this digest
        /// (the tag only if it resolves to it now). A reference it cannot
        /// confirm is `invalid-request`.
        image: string,
        /// An OCI digest, `sha256:<hex>`.
        digest: string,
    }

    /// A named volume mounted into one component.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record volume-mount {
        /// A volume name the implementer resolves in the caller's scope;
        /// never a host path. The implementer compares the storage it
        /// resolves to, never the name, and an approval binds that storage.
        volume: string,
        /// Where the component sees the volume.
        mount-path: string,
        /// True mounts the volume read-only. Read-write counts toward both
        /// read and egress reach.
        read-only: bool,
    }

    /// An interface granted to one component. The implementer, not the
    /// request, classifies it as reading, writing, or both.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record interface-grant {
        /// Fully qualified interface name with its version. A
        /// `cosmonic:agent` interface is `invalid-request`: those powers come
        /// only through `agent-powers` or the workload's own manifest.
        name: string,
        /// Interface-specific configuration, such as a bucket or topic.
        config: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// What one component may touch. Empty grants nothing; nothing is
    /// inherited from the caller.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record grants {
        /// Hosts this component may reach.
        allowed-hosts: list<string>,
        /// Volumes mounted into this component.
        volumes: list<volume-mount>,
        /// Interfaces this component may import.
        interfaces: list<interface-grant>,
        /// Secret names, never values, that the implementer delivers into
        /// this component's memory. Refused on a spawned agent's own
        /// component; use `credentials` for authenticated egress.
        secrets: list<string>,
        /// Credential references by logical name. The implementer resolves
        /// each through a binding the operator scoped to this workload and
        /// applies it to the component's outbound requests, owning those
        /// authentication headers; the value never enters the component.
        /// Without a credential broker, every reference is beyond the
        /// ceiling.
        credentials: list<string>,
    }

    /// A toolset: one component, with its own grants, that offers one or
    /// more tools. Used for a child's toolsets in `spawn` and for a load in
    /// `tool-loading`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record toolset-spec {
        /// A DNS label of at most 32 characters (lowercase letters, digits
        /// and `-`, starting and ending with a letter or digit), unique
        /// among one agent's toolsets. Display metadata and the default
        /// alias prefix; routing and authorization use the toolset's
        /// component identity and digest.
        name: string,
        /// A tool provider (exports `cosmonic:agent/tools`) or a typed tool
        /// (exports its own interfaces).
        component: component-ref,
        /// What this toolset component may touch.
        grants: grants,
        /// The functions to offer, by name; none offers all. Trims a large
        /// component so it spends less of the model's function budget. A
        /// name the component does not have is `invalid-request`.
        functions: option<list<string>>,
        /// How this toolset's tools are named to the model. None: each tool
        /// keeps its own name unless an alias in the session already has it,
        /// and is then `<name>__<tool>`. Some(prefix): every tool is the
        /// prefix plus its own name, so `""` keeps own names and `"gh__"`
        /// gives `gh__search`. An alias already taken, or one outside model
        /// tool-name rules (only `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, at most 64 characters), is
        /// `invalid-request`; the implementer never renames. An operator
        /// setting: refused in a caller's request unless the operator lets
        /// callers choose prefixes.
        alias-prefix: option<string>,
    }

    /// What may come back to the caller. Covered by the approval digest.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record output-spec {
        /// JSON Schema that `structured` must satisfy. None means reply
        /// parts only.
        schema: option<json>,
        /// Maximum encoded size of one run's outcome, in bytes.
        max-bytes: u32,
        /// True returns only the validated `structured` value: the
        /// implementer empties `reply` and builds every other outcome field
        /// from its own records. Requires `schema`, or the request is
        /// `invalid-request`. For reading untrusted content in quarantine.
        /// The implementer does not check the schema for free strings.
        structured-only: bool,
    }

    /// What a child may spend. Covered by the approval digest. A none field
    /// takes the implementer's default, which policy may cap. The
    /// implementer enforces it, not the child: it refuses a run past
    /// `max-runs`, ends a run at `max-duration`, and meters model calls
    /// itself. A child's spend also counts against every ancestor's, and
    /// every root session has a finite token and wall-clock budget.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record budget {
        /// Runs the child may take, the first included.
        max-runs: option<u32>,
        /// Model calls per run (`max_turns` in the OpenAI Agents SDK, the
        /// Claude Agent SDK and Goose). Passed to the child as
        /// `run-options.max-steps` and enforced by metering.
        max-steps: option<u32>,
        /// Tokens across all runs: the sum of `usage.prompt-tokens` and
        /// `usage.completion-tokens`.
        max-total-tokens: option<u64>,
        /// Wall-clock time from spawn. Passing it ends any run in progress
        /// with finish `max-duration`, and ends the child's session.
        max-duration: option<duration>,
    }

    /// Powers a child may receive. Empty grants none. A child never
    /// inherits powers, and receives one only if the caller holds it.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    flags agent-powers {
        /// The child may start child sessions of its own, within the tree
        /// limits.
        spawn,
        /// The child may call `tool-discovery.list`.
        tool-discovery,
        /// The child may load toolsets into itself, bounded by its
        /// ancestors' ceilings.
        tool-loading,
    }

    /// Which agent a child session runs.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant agent-source {
        /// A component targeting `cosmonic:agent/runnable-agent`, with the
        /// request's own model alias, grants and toolsets.
        component(component-ref),
        /// The name of an agent definition in the operator's catalog, which
        /// supplies the component, model alias, grants and toolsets. The
        /// request's `model-alias`, `grants` and `toolsets` must be empty,
        /// or it is `invalid-request`. Admitted by the same rules as a
        /// component: a catalog entry is not a privilege. The admission
        /// snapshot resolves the name to the definition's content, so a
        /// changed definition is a new request.
        definition(string),
    }

    /// Where the host should run a child. A hint: the host may choose
    /// otherwise, and reports where the child ran in `spawn.child`.
    /// Placement never changes reach: a child runs only where its admission
    /// snapshot resolves identically (the same storage behind its volumes,
    /// the same credential bindings), and a hint that cannot be honoured
    /// without changing reach is ignored. A child whose grants, or its
    /// toolsets', name a host whose meaning depends on location (loopback,
    /// link-local, private-range, single-label or cluster-local) runs on the
    /// parent's host whatever the hint. The audit entry records the
    /// placement.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant placement {
        /// The host chooses.
        any,
        /// The parent's own host.
        same-host,
        /// A host group the operator named.
        host-group(string),
        /// A zone or region the operator named.
        zone(string),
        /// Reserved for later versions. `invalid-request` as caller input.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Everything needed to start a child agent session. Its toolsets are
    /// fixed at spawn and change only through `tool-loading`, if the child
    /// holds that power. Construction is lenient; `spawn` validates and
    /// returns `invalid-request`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record spawn-request {
        /// The agent the child runs.
        agent: agent-source,
        /// A model alias, as `models.open` takes it, that the caller was
        /// granted (a child holds only its own). Counts toward read reach.
        model-alias: string,
        /// The child's instructions. Caller free text: not covered by the
        /// approval digest and never shown in its summary.
        instructions: string,
        /// What the agent component itself may touch. Must fit the caller's
        /// own grants.
        grants: grants,
        /// The child's toolsets, in order; the order assigns aliases. Names
        /// must be unique.
        toolsets: list<toolset-spec>,
        /// What may come back, and how much.
        output: output-spec,
        /// What the child may spend over its lifetime.
        budget: budget,
        /// Powers the child receives.
        powers: agent-powers,
        /// Where to run it; none is `any`. Not covered by the approval
        /// digest.
        placement: option<placement>,
        /// For logs, audit and display, and returned by `spawn.children`.
        /// Never policy input, and not covered by the approval digest.
        labels: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// Where a toolset stands in the caller's session.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant toolset-state {
        /// Admitted, with its rollout not finished: not callable yet. If the
        /// rollout fails or passes its deadline (the operator's, recommended
        /// two minutes), the toolset leaves `loaded()` and an identical
        /// `load` tries again. Cannot be set aside until `offered`.
        loading,
        /// In the model's tool list and callable.
        offered,
        /// Bound and approved, but set aside out of the model's tool list to
        /// stay within the function budget. A `load` of the same spec
        /// returns it to `offered` at once, with no rollout. Its aliases
        /// answer `unknown-tool` with how to bring it back.
        ready,
        /// A state not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A grantor's decision on a request that needed approval.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant decision {
        /// Granted. For a load, the toolset joins the session ceiling and any
        /// identical load admits it. For a spawn, resend the identical
        /// request with the same key; the grant admits one child.
        granted,
        /// Denied, with an implementer-authored reason. Identical requests
        /// from the same caller answer `denied`, with no new prompt, for the
        /// host's denial cooldown.
        denied(string),
        /// No decision before the approval expired, or the implementer
        /// withdrew it (its snapshot no longer matches, or the person removed
        /// the toolset). Not a denial: an identical request raises a new
        /// approval.
        expired,
        /// A decision not captured above. Treat as denied.
        other(string),
    }

    /// An earlier admission of the same digest from the same caller, which
    /// makes a new approval a repeat.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record prior-admission {
        /// The id of the approval whose grant admitted it.
        approval-id: string,
        /// How long before this approval was raised it was admitted.
        admitted-ago: duration,
        /// True while the child that admission started has not ended.
        still-running: bool,
    }

    /// A request sent to a grantor. The approval is host state: it stays
    /// pending until decided or expired, whether or not anyone holds this
    /// handle. Dropping the handle only stops waiting.
    ///
    /// Coalescing. While an approval is pending, identical requests from the
    /// same caller return it (one id, one prompt), and every waiter sees the
    /// same decision.
    ///
    /// Loads. A granted load adds the toolset to the session ceiling at once;
    /// there is no separate ticket. The "already held" rule in
    /// `tool-loading` keeps concurrent identical loads to one rollout.
    ///
    /// Spawns. A granted spawn is a one-time ticket for one child, consumed
    /// atomically by the first resend's idempotency key; later resends with
    /// that key return the same child. Consuming it checks, in one step, the
    /// grant's expiry (the operator's lifetime), a fresh snapshot, current
    /// policy, every ancestor's current ceiling, and that no removal since
    /// the grant touched a toolset it names. Coalesced waiters with other
    /// keys get a new approval, marked as a repeat (`get-repeat-of`). The
    /// approved toolsets become the child's own entries (see Children on
    /// `spawn-types`).
    ///
    /// Admission snapshot. Before raising an approval, the implementer
    /// resolves the request into an immutable snapshot: the storage behind
    /// each volume; the binding behind each credential reference (what it
    /// authenticates as and to, not the secret, so rotation within a binding
    /// changes nothing); the secret each name resolves to; the model alias's
    /// provider, model and endpoint; an agent definition's content; each
    /// interface grant's resource and classification; every executable
    /// digest; and every default the request left open (budget fields left
    /// none and configured operator limits, never live counters). The
    /// person's approval UI shows it and the audit log records it; the
    /// caller's summary carries at most an opaque, keyed fingerprint.
    /// Admission re-resolves and compares: any difference spends a spawn
    /// ticket or voids a session ceiling entry, and the request asks again.
    /// A pending approval whose names now resolve differently is withdrawn
    /// (`expired`) and one new approval is raised.
    ///
    /// Cooldown and caps. After a denial, `other`, or the person removing the
    /// toolset, matching requests answer `denied` with no prompt for the
    /// host's cooldown (recommended one hour). Matching is by reach, not by
    /// name: the same executable digests and resolved reach anywhere in the
    /// spawn tree, so a fresh child, a renamed toolset or a changed
    /// `max-bytes` cannot ask again at once. Pending approvals are capped per
    /// caller (recommended three) and per tree (recommended five), each
    /// operator-set and at least one; past either cap a request answers
    /// `over-limit` with scope `pending-approvals` and raises no prompt.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    resource approval {
        /// Implementer-issued id, to correlate with the approval UI and the
        /// audit log.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-id: func() -> string;

        /// `sha256:<hex>` over a domain tag naming the operation and its
        /// version (`cosmonic:agent/spawn-request@3` or
        /// `cosmonic:agent/toolset-spec@3`), the caller's workload
        /// incarnation, the caller's component name, the caller's session
        /// id, and the request in canonical JSON, each separated from the
        /// next by a zero byte. Compare for equality only.
        ///
        /// It covers exactly the fields the summary shows: the whole
        /// `toolset-spec` for a load; every `spawn-request` field except
        /// `instructions`, `placement` and `labels` for a spawn.
        ///
        /// Normalization, before digesting and before the ceiling check:
        /// `allowed-hosts` entries are parsed, scheme and host lowercased, an
        /// internationalized host converted to its A-label form, one trailing
        /// dot stripped, and a default port (80 for `http` and `ws`, 443 for
        /// `https` and `wss`) dropped; an entry that does not parse is
        /// `invalid-request`. Set lists (`allowed-hosts`, `secrets`,
        /// `credentials`, `volumes`, `interfaces`, `functions`) drop exact
        /// duplicates and are sorted by the UTF-8 bytes of each element's
        /// canonical JSON; two different elements with one key (two volumes
        /// at one `mount-path`, two interface grants with one `name`) are
        /// `invalid-request`. `toolsets` keeps its order. Names, digests and
        /// paths are compared as given.
        ///
        /// Each WIT value as JSON, serialized by RFC 8785 (JCS): a record is
        /// an object with every field, named as in this WIT; a string, bool
        /// or u32 is itself; u64 and `duration` are strings of decimal digits;
        /// an option is `null` or the value; a list is an array, and
        /// `list<tuple<string, string>>` is an object (a repeated key is
        /// `invalid-request`); flags are a sorted array of set names; a
        /// variant is its case name, or a one-member object `{"case":
        /// payload}`; `json` is embedded as parsed JSON.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-request-digest: func() -> string;

        /// Implementer-authored text for the person who decides: what is
        /// declassified ("results of this agent, schema S, at most N bytes,
        /// return to <caller>"), whether a toolset joins the session ceiling
        /// (a load) or serves this one child only (a spawn), what a load
        /// would set aside, and where the requesting session sits in the
        /// tree. A repeat says so first. Built only from values the
        /// implementer validated: catalog names, confirmed image references,
        /// and caller-chosen names shown as quoted identifiers limited to
        /// `[a-z0-9-_./:]`. Never carries caller free text or resolved
        /// infrastructure detail.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-summary: func() -> string;

        /// For spawns only: the most recent admission of a spawn with this
        /// digest from the same caller within the repeat window (the
        /// operator's, recommended one hour, extended while that child has
        /// not ended).
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-repeat-of: func() -> option<prior-admission>;

        /// Resolves when the grantor decides. Dropping the future stops
        /// waiting and does not withdraw the request.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        wait-for-decision: async func() -> decision;
    }

    /// Which implementer limit a request would exceed.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant limit-scope {
        /// Child sessions with a run in progress at once in one spawn tree
        /// (the root session and every descendant). Clears when one ends.
        /// Default 20.
        concurrent-agents,
        /// Nesting depth, where a child of a session a person or trigger
        /// started is at depth 1. Does not clear. Default 3.
        depth,
        /// Child sessions spawned in total by one spawn tree, ended ones
        /// included. Does not clear. Default 500.
        total-agents,
        /// Memory the implementer reserves for a child.
        memory,
        /// Toolset components bound in one agent's workload at once, set
        /// aside ones included. Clears when one is unbound. Default 16.
        loaded-toolsets,
        /// Functions offered to the model at once, when nothing can be set
        /// aside to make room. The budget comes from the model binding.
        offered-functions,
        /// Approvals pending at once, per caller and per spawn tree. Always
        /// set. Clears when one is decided or expires.
        pending-approvals,
        /// Child sessions not yet ended in one spawn tree. Always set.
        /// Clears when one ends. Default 50.
        live-sessions,
        /// A scope not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// An implementer limit a request would exceed.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record over-limit {
        /// Which limit.
        scope: limit-scope,
        /// The limit's value, where the implementer reports it.
        limit: option<u64>,
    }

    /// Why a spawn admitted no child, or a load added no toolset. No child
    /// session was created and no toolset became callable, though start-up
    /// code run by a failed rollout is not rolled back. Detail strings are
    /// implementer-authored and diagnostic only.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant admission-error {
        /// This power is not enabled on this host, or not granted to this
        /// caller. Permanent for this caller: adapt rather than retry.
        disabled,
        /// A policy or a person refused, or the request reaches beyond what
        /// the caller's place in the tree allows. A different request may
        /// succeed. Also returned, with no prompt, during a denial cooldown.
        denied(string),
        /// Sent to a grantor, and returned at once so the caller's run is
        /// never held. Wait for the decision, then resend the identical
        /// request.
        needs-approval(approval),
        /// An implementer limit would be exceeded.
        over-limit(over-limit),
        /// Malformed: a bad digest, a duplicate toolset name, a name held by
        /// a different toolset, an unknown model alias, definition or
        /// function, or a component that does not target the right world.
        invalid-request(string),
        /// The implementer could not serve the call, or the rollout failed
        /// and left the caller's toolsets as they were. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a run is paused. Implementer-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant blocked-reason {
        /// A tool call or a load waits on a person or the auditor.
        tool-approval,
        /// Policy holds the outcome until a person or the auditor releases it.
        outcome-release,
        /// The child's last run lost its host, and the next run waits out
        /// the re-placement delay before it starts.
        placement-delay,
        /// A reason not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Contentless progress. The implementer coalesces frames and never
    /// blocks on an unread stream.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant progress {
        /// The child is working.
        working,
        /// The run is paused.
        blocked(blocked-reason),
        /// Cancellation was requested and the child has not stopped yet;
        /// `spawn.send` answers `busy` until it has.
        cancelling,
        /// Cumulative counters for this run so far, from the implementer's
        /// own metering.
        counts(run-counts),
        /// A frame not captured above. Safe to ignore.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A budget item that can leave a child unable to take another run.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant budget-item {
        /// `budget.max-runs` runs were accepted.
        max-runs,
        /// `budget.max-total-tokens` were spent.
        max-total-tokens,
        /// `budget.max-duration` passed.
        max-duration,
        /// An item not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a child's run did not start; nothing reached the child. Unlike
    /// the `run` export's `start-error`, it reports budget and tree limits
    /// and never says `duplicate`, since the implementer mints the child's
    /// run ids.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant start-error {
        /// A run is in progress, or a cancelled one has not stopped yet.
        busy,
        /// No session with that id is a direct child of the caller.
        unknown,
        /// The child's admission no longer fits its ancestors' current
        /// ceilings or policy, so it takes no more runs.
        denied(string),
        /// Accepting the run would exceed a tree limit
        /// (`concurrent-agents`). May clear when another run ends.
        over-limit(over-limit),
        /// A budget item is spent; the child takes no more runs.
        exhausted(budget-item),
        /// The message cannot be taken. A different message may succeed.
        invalid-input(string),
        /// The child's session has ended (by `end`, its `max-duration`, or
        /// its parent's end) and will never accept another run.
        ended,
        /// The run could not be accepted now (no host could take it, or the
        /// session store could not be reached). Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a child's run failed after it started. Detail strings are the
    /// implementer's, never the child's; the child's own typed errors reach
    /// the caller only as `failed`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant run-error {
        /// The run was cancelled (`spawn.cancel` or `spawn.end`).
        /// Cooperative: effects caused before the child stopped are not
        /// rolled back.
        cancelled,
        /// The outcome failed `output.schema` or exceeded `output.max-bytes`.
        output-rejected(string),
        /// A person or the auditor declined to release the outcome.
        withheld,
        /// The host running the child went away mid-run (its lease in the
        /// session store lapsed). Effects before that are not rolled back.
        /// The child's session survives; a later `send` starts a new run.
        host-lost(string),
        /// The child failed. Details are in the host's view of its session.
        failed(string),
        /// The run ended and its outcome is no longer kept (for a run
        /// returned again by an idempotency key, or waited on late).
        expired,
        /// The implementer could not finish serving the run.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A child's started run: contentless progress, then the outcome. Unlike
    /// the `run` export's stream, the child's text and steps never reach the
    /// caller. The stream closes before the outcome resolves. Dropping either
    /// only stops listening: only `spawn.cancel` or `spawn.end` cancels, and
    /// `spawn.wait` collects the outcome later by run id. With
    /// `output.structured-only`, `reply` is empty and every other field is
    /// the implementer's own.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    type started-run = tuple<stream<progress>, future<result<run-outcome, run-error>>>;
}
